  "prompt_text": "The figure uses a mouse tumor model to summarize how the tumor microenvironment shapes cancer progression, diagnosis, and treatment. At the center is a cross-section of a tumor showing several visible barriers: compressed blood and lymphatic vessels, a stiff and excessive extracellular matrix, and a cancer glycocalyx. These features appear to restrict drug entry, highlighted by reduced drug accumulation near the tumor, while also supporting tumor growth through growth factors and angiogenesis.\n\nThe same abnormal environment is linked to metastatic spread, with tumor cells leaving as circulating tumor cell clusters and with exosomes released into the circulation. On the right, these circulating materials are sampled by liquid biopsy, suggesting minimally invasive monitoring of disease. Below, a microfabrication device is shown as a tool for isolating CTC clusters and exosomes, and for building organs-on-chips or engineered tumor models.\n\nThe figure also connects this biology to clinical and experimental technologies. Imaging methods, including MRI, intravital microscopy, imaging probes, PET, and CT, are positioned over the mouse to indicate cellular and molecular imaging of tumors in vivo. Drug delivery approaches such as RNAi therapies, immunotherapies, and implantable devices are shown on the left, emphasizing efforts to overcome the tumor’s physical and molecular barriers.",
